# TypeScript Coding Standards

## Type Safety Rules

### Strict TypeScript Configuration
- Always use strict TypeScript configuration
- Enable `strict: true` in tsconfig.json
- Use `noImplicitAny: true` to catch missing type annotations
- Enable `strictNullChecks: true` to handle null/undefined properly

### Type Definitions
- Never use `any` type - use proper types or `unknown`
- Define interfaces for all props and API responses
- Use proper return types for all functions
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use discriminated unions for complex state management

### Common Type Patterns
```typescript
// Good: Proper interface definition
interface StudentOrderProps {
  orderId: string;
  studentName: string;
  totalAmount: number;
  orderDate: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
}

// Good: Discriminated union
type OrderStatus = 'pending' | 'approved' | 'preparing' | 'ready' | 'served' | 'cancelled';

// Bad: Using any
function processOrder(order: any) { ... }

// Good: Proper typing
function processOrder(order: StudentOrderProps): Promise<OrderResponse> { ... }
```

### Error Handling Types
- Use proper error types for API responses
- Implement Result<T, E> pattern for better error handling
- Use union types for expected errors

```typescript
// Good: Proper error typing
type OrderResult = 
  | { success: true; data: Order }
  | { success: false; error: string };

// API response interface
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: string;
  message?: string;
}
```

### Component Props
- Always define interfaces for React component props
- Use optional properties with `?` when appropriate
- Provide default values for optional props

```typescript
// Good: Proper component props
interface MenuItemCardProps {
  item: MenuItem;
  onAddToCart: (itemId: string) => void;
  isInCart?: boolean;
  quantity?: number;
}

const MenuItemCard: React.FC<MenuItemCardProps> = ({
  item,
  onAddToCart,
  isInCart = false,
  quantity = 0
}) => {
  // Component implementation
};
```

### Database Types
- Use Prisma generated types for database operations
- Create proper types for database queries
- Use type-safe database operations

```typescript
// Good: Using Prisma types
import { Order, OrderItem, MenuItem } from '@prisma/client';

type OrderWithItems = Order & {
  orderItems: (OrderItem & {
    menuItem: MenuItem;
  })[];
};
```

### API Route Types
- Define proper types for API request/response
- Use type guards for runtime validation
- Implement proper error response types

```typescript
// Good: API route types
interface CreateOrderRequest {
  items: {
    menuItemId: string;
    quantity: number;
  }[];
  deliveryDate: string;
  notes?: string;
}

interface CreateOrderResponse {
  orderId: string;
  totalAmount: number;
  orderNumber: string;
}
```

### Type Guards and Validation
- Use type guards for runtime type checking
- Implement proper validation functions
- Use Zod for schema validation

```typescript
// Good: Type guard
function isValidOrderStatus(status: string): status is OrderStatus {
  return ['pending', 'approved', 'preparing', 'ready', 'served', 'cancelled'].includes(status);
}

// Good: Zod validation
import { z } from 'zod';

const OrderSchema = z.object({
  items: z.array(z.object({
    menuItemId: z.string(),
    quantity: z.number().min(1)
  })),
  deliveryDate: z.string().datetime(),
  notes: z.string().optional()
});
```

### Common TypeScript Mistakes to Avoid
- Don't use `any` type
- Don't ignore TypeScript compiler warnings
- Don't use `@ts-ignore` without proper justification
- Don't use loose equality (`==`) - use strict equality (`===`)
- Don't forget to handle null/undefined cases
- Don't use `var` - use `const` or `let`

### Best Practices
- Use descriptive type names
- Keep types close to where they're used
- Use generic types for reusable components
- Implement proper type inference
- Use readonly for immutable data
- Use const assertions for literal types
